.TH UAC 4 "June 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
uac \- USB Audio Class playback driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <drivers/usb/usb.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B uac
driver binds USB Audio Class (UAC) functions and exposes their playback
path through the
.BR audio (4)
framework as a
.B /dev/audioN
device, so the ordinary audio stack \(em
.BR mpg123 ,
.BR /dev/audio
writers, the
.B audioio
ioctls \(em drives a USB headset or DAC.
.PP
The driver supports playback (host-to-device) only; capture endpoints are
ignored.  It handles both UAC 1.0 and UAC 2.0 functions that present a
single sample clock and a 16-bit alternate setting.
.SS Operation
When an audio function is enumerated, the driver:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Walks the configuration descriptor for the AudioStreaming interface's
playback alternate setting, preferring a 16-bit format (matching the
framework's signed-16 path), and locates its isochronous OUT endpoint and
.IR wMaxPacketSize .
.IP 2. 4
Locates the clock entity feeding the USB-streaming input terminal and,
for UAC 2.0, sets it to 48 kHz with a clock-source
.B SET_CUR
request.
.IP 3. 4
Leaves the device's Feature Units untouched.  Some hardware \(em notably the
Apple EarPods \(em latches into a sticky microphone-monitor state on
.I any
Feature Unit write, silencing USB playback until it is physically re-plugged,
and Linux's
.B snd-usb-audio
likewise programs only the clock and the alternate setting.  Playback volume is
applied in software instead (see
.B Volume
below).
.IP 4. 4
Issues
.B SET_INTERFACE
to activate the alternate setting, bringing the isochronous endpoint
live.
.IP 5. 4
Registers an
.BR audio (4)
device whose
.BR write (2)
streams PCM straight to the endpoint.
.RE
.SS Isochronous Transfer
Audio is carried over an isochronous OUT endpoint: one packet per 1 ms
frame at full speed, no handshake and no retry.  Because the endpoint is
isochronous-synchronous (the device's DAC clock is fixed), the host must
feed exactly one packet every frame, continuously, or playback stutters.
The driver therefore buffers
.BR write (2)
data in a deep software FIFO and runs a feeder kernel thread that keeps a
sliding window of iso packets scheduled a few frames ahead of the
controller (through the host controller's
.IR iso_schedule / iso_reclaim
operations).  This decouples bursty writes from the steady iso clock, so
the stream is gapless between writes and across decode jitter.
.SS Format
The device is presented to userland as 48 kHz, signed 16-bit
little-endian, stereo, and runs its DAC at a fixed 48 kHz.  Source PCM at
any sample rate is resampled to 48 kHz with a linear interpolator before
streaming, so a 44.1 kHz source (e.g. a typical MP3) plays at the correct
pitch.  Input is assumed to be 16-bit stereo.
.SS Volume
Playback volume is applied in software, because writing the device's Feature
Unit volume control is unsafe on some hardware (see
.B Operation
step 3).  The
.BR audio (4)
.B play.gain
field (0\(en255, set with the
.B AUDIO_SETINFO
ioctl or
.BR audioctl (1))
scales every sample before it is sent down the wire.  The dial is mapped to the
linear sample factor with a square-law taper \(em a fixed-point approximation of
a logarithmic (decibel) control, since perceived loudness is logarithmic and a
linear map would crowd the usable range into the top of the dial.  The multiply
is performed on the 24-bit sample (after resampling and the 16-to-24-bit
expansion) so attenuation does not discard the low bits of a 16-bit source.
Volume defaults to full scale until
.B play.gain
is set.
.SH HARDWARE
The driver matches any device whose interface class is
.B USB_CLASS_AUDIO
with an AudioControl interface, independent of UAC protocol version.  It
has been developed against the Apple EarPods
.RB ( 05ac:110b ),
a full-speed UAC 2.0 headset whose playback path is interface 1,
alternate setting 2 (16-bit/48 kHz/stereo) on isochronous OUT endpoint
.BR 0x02 .
.SH NOTES
Only full-speed isochronous OUT is implemented; isochronous IN
(capture) and feedback endpoints are not.  Resampling is a simple linear
interpolator (adequate for playback, not a polyphase / band-limited
converter), and the input is assumed to be 16-bit stereo.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/audioN
The audio sample device registered for the bound function.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audio (4),
.BR write (2)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B uac
driver and the UHCI isochronous transfer path it depends on were added
together to bring USB audio playback to Substrate.
